\begin{abstract}
We construct an explicit bridge between two structures that arise independently
in the semantics of neural architectures and in the algebraic geometry of proof
systems. A \emph{neural observation system} is a deterministic state machine
$(\step,\observe)$ whose behavioral (Myhill--Nerode) equivalence collapses
internal states that no input word can distinguish; quotienting by it yields the
canonical minimal realization, i.e.\ certified compression. A \emph{semiring
congruence} is the basic datum of proof-theoretic algebraic geometry, whose
prime instances are the points of a proof spectrum. We isolate the minimal
hypothesis under which these worlds meet: an \emph{algebraic neural observation
system}, whose state and output spaces are semirings and whose layers and
read-out are semiring maps preserving $0$, $+$, and $\cdot$ (but \emph{not} the
multiplicative unit). Under this hypothesis the behavior map $x \mapsto (w
\mapsto \behav(x,w))$ is a semiring homomorphism in its state argument, so
behavioral equivalence is a genuine \emph{semiring congruence} $\behavCong$, and
the assignment $N \mapsto \behavCong(N)$ is \emph{functorial} along intertwining
morphisms. We realize the same kernel analytically as the zero-set of an
\emph{observation pseudometric} $\obsDist$, prove $\obsDist$ is a pseudometric,
and prove the keystone identity: the metric kernel equals the semiring
congruence. We further identify the kernel with the intersection of the depth-$k$
partition-refinement filtration. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Two classical impulses meet in this paper. The first is the \textbf{Myhill--Nerode}
impulse: understand a state machine through the equivalence that identifies states
no observation can separate, and study the quotient as the canonical minimal
realization. In the neural setting this models \emph{certified compression} ---
discarding redundant internal degrees of freedom while preserving every
observable output. The second is the \textbf{proof-spectrum} impulse: treat a
proof or computation system as a semiring (disjunction as $+$, conjunction as
$\cdot$), and develop an algebraic geometry of its \emph{congruences} in analogy
with the Zariski geometry of ideals; the prime congruences are geometric points.

Our contribution is to make these impulses literally the same object under a
minimal hypothesis. We show that behavioral equivalence of an \emph{algebraic}
neural observation system is a \textbf{semiring congruence}, that the assignment
is \textbf{functorial}, and that the congruence is simultaneously the zero-set of
an \textbf{observation pseudometric} and the limit of the depth filtration used by
partition refinement.

